back to our panel Tom Bevan  RealClearPolitics co-founder and  publisher Molly Hemingway senior editor  at the Federalists and Mike Allen  co-founder and executive editor of Axios  Molly what perked your ears on all of  that well my colleague Mike Allen here  cheerfully said that Moeller is unafraid  and it's worth remembering that he was  also unafraid when he botched the  anthrax investigation by targeting the  wrong person a blunder that cost  taxpayers quite a bit of money I think  at this point there is definitely a  burden of proof that the FBI Department  of Justice and Special Counsel have to  meet President Trump talked about some  of the conflicts of interests that are  there and Rosenstein actually has quite  a few of them not just what he was cited  for with the letter recommending the  firing of Comey he also was probably the  guy who signed off on Andrew McCabe's  ridiculous investigation into whether  sessions was a Russian spy and he also  is overseeing this u.s. AG who's  investigating FISA abuses of which  Rosenstein was also involved there are  plenty of conflicts going around it  would be good for the media to be  thinking a little bit more about these  conflicts the conflicts in the special  counsel with so many Democrats all of  the problems that we've seen arise out  of what has been able to be pulled there  and it's worth remembering that Congress  has been requesting for a long time just  to find out how did this investigation  into Russia start and the Department of  Justice and FBI are absolutely  stonewalling and obstructing that just  today 3,600 documents go into the House  Judiciary Committee so it's important to  realize there are a lot of moving parts  here on a number of different  investigations both of the FBI and DOJ  and obviously of whatever special  counselor Muller is looking into yeah  and the president there mentioning a  million documents I thought that was new  there's so much news these days they've  said that's 1.4 million documents have  gone from the White House to Muller but  just to hit pause for a second on that  amazing moment that we saw play out the  live play out like that will be picked  over for years look at the president's  body language listen to his tone listen  to his repetition using disgrace  disgraceful again and again saying twice  we'll see what happens John Roberts so  good on what happens when he says that  about you so the president fighting and  this really plays into what he's been  trying to do for a while we've seen the  discrepancy and his lawyers strategy and  his  the lawyers have wanted to cooperate  told him assured him that it would pay  off for him to cooperate the president's  been resisting he's been wanted to fight  he's been wanting to discredit the molar  findings with his base and this does it  big time you know it's interesting just  to think about what exactly and we don't  know what Muller may have found that  referred to the US attorney in New York  but having the politics of all of this  swirl around at the same time meanwhile  Tom this is one week ahead of Jim  Comey's book that's coming out right it  is as these investigations are going on  on Capitol Hill Muller has to know that  he crosses a line in at least the  president's mind as he's negotiating  with the president to sit down by doing  this so you wonder how you get to that  point yeah look I thought the  President's speech wasn't me it was it  was a Greatest Hits I mean he went  through combing Hillary Clinton lies  jeff Sessions recusal molar conflict  Rosen Stein I mean it was it was a  recitation of the greatest hits  obviously he's upset and as britt hume  mentioned earlier the only crime that we  know of that might be connected with us  right now is an FEC violation and so you  know over here Trump starting with  collusion with Russia and now we're  looking at a private attorney private  attorney of the president being raided  by the FBI no-knock raid it's a pretty  said early it's a pretty dramatic  increase in the in the stakes of this  investigation and I would not be  surprised the other thing that everyone  has told Trump not to do is to fire Bob  Mona and and I suspect he might be going  on his own gut now and thinking you know  what I've been right on this all along I  might want to do it so I mean just put  this in context this is a live  presidential statement about a raid on  his personal attorney's office that  deals with communications were told that  had tied to an alleged affair with a  porn star and a payoff days before the  election as he sits there and  contemplates whether to strike Syria and  retaliation for a chemical weapons  attack I just want to put that in  perspective  as people are getting their head around  all this and there is that subtext to  that the president talked about which is  there has been an active resistance  movement since the moment he was elected  and they kind of go from item to item in  an attempt to bring him down so that is  part of the context there and he kind of  unlike a lot of presidents he's so  transparent and how he speaks he just  talks about what is actually going on in  this moment but it's worth people  thinking about as they do their  resistance which is you know you're  right as an American citizen to do  whether it's worth bringing instability  at this time for some of these issues  going on one more pivotal thing the  president quadrupling down on fire and  called me remember Steve Bannon said  that was the biggest mistake in recent  political history the president saying  twice there he was right and that fits  with what you were saying about how he's  now feeling about Bob mark although  referencing rod Rosen Stein's letter  saying this is what should happen  whether that was written you know after  and how and when well the entire  question I think right now is either  there is treasonous collusion between  Trump and Russia or there is an FBI and  Department of Justice that is completely  out of control you think it's one of the  two well I would love to see any  evidence for the first claim that we  have been spending so much time on we  have a lot of evidence to support the  second claim the Washington Post at this  hour is saying that the the raids dealt  with FEC violations and bank fraud we  have no way to confirm that if that's  possible and and it's separate it seems  like it's separate from Muller and he's  referring something that he finds this  is all speculation until you find out  what they're looking at right as I said  earlier when you have a news breaking  news event like this there's the fog of  war a lot of questions are raised we  don't have a lot of answers to them it  will become clear in the coming you know  days and weeks exactly what again who  proved this how did the process go how  did we end up it's kind of unusual for  Muller to bounce this up to the district  attorney in New York or to have Rosen  saying sort of given the approval to do  that and then again what was the after  what did he find what was he looking for  and how is that connected to Trump in  any way or if it's not you know then is  Michael Cohen just collateral damage is  little in this whole affair Brett that  Washington Post Bulletin a single source  but from a reliable  porter and a reminder that this  investigation is moving so much faster  than a special counseling usually does  and faster than we thought this was  moving all right in the in the other  politics here you heard him the  president talked about military funding  funding for shoring up the military as  he considers military action now he is  trying to get congress to rescind some  of the 1.3 trillion dollar spending bill  that he signed and actually threatened  to veto at one point that when they were  going to pass it and the concern about  the CBO projections which we're going to  get into from from capitol hill all of  this playing on the same day yeah and  rescission is this idea of rescinding  some of that spending is important not  just because republicans have liked to  think of themselves as fiscally  responsible and this 1.3 trillion dollar  omnibus was anything but fiscally  responsible the base of the party is  very upset with that kind of spending  spree and there is an option where you  only need a simple majority in congress  in the senate to rescind some of that  bloated budget and the leaders in the  house and the senate can just suggest to  donald trump what he suggests they  rescind and get those votes through that  would be not just good for the fiscal  health of the country but it's  absolutely important for a base that is  wondering why have republicans in each  branch if they are not going to actually  govern as they claim they will

